WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.448
Safe Rust prevents ordinary
use-after-free,

2
00:00:02.528 --> 00:00:04.825
but unsafe code and FFI boundaries require
extra care.

3
00:00:04.905 --> 00:00:08.401
Raw pointers do not carry the same checked
lifetime guarantees as safe references.

4
00:00:08.481 --> 00:00:10.529
External libraries may allocate, free,
retain,

5
00:00:10.609 --> 00:00:13.206
or call back into Rust using rules the
compiler cannot verify.

6
00:00:13.286 --> 00:00:16.033
Review should cover pointer validity and
lifetime assumptions.

7
00:00:16.113 --> 00:00:17.861
Is the pointer still valid when it is
used?

8
00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:18.790
Who owns the memory?

9
00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:19.619
Who allocated it?

10
00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:20.348
Who may free it?

11
00:00:20.428 --> 00:00:22.976
Can external code retain the pointer after
the call returns?

12
00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:26.352
Can Rust continue using a value after
ownership has moved across the boundary?

13
00:00:26.432 --> 00:00:28.480
Callbacks are a common lifetime pressure
point.

14
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.857
External code may store a callback and
invoke it later.

15
00:00:30.937 --> 00:00:34.233
The callback may depend on Rust state that
could be dropped before invocation.

16
00:00:34.313 --> 00:00:37.210
A safe design needs a clear registration,
ownership, cancellation,

17
00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.087
and cleanup story so callback use cannot
outlive referenced data.

18
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.864
Allocation and deallocation pairs deserve
the same attention.

19
00:00:42.944 --> 00:00:45.241
If Rust allocates memory and another
library frees it,

20
00:00:45.321 --> 00:00:48.268
the contract must say that explicitly and
use compatible mechanisms.

21
00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.745
If external code allocates memory and
returns it to Rust,

22
00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:54.271
the wrapper must know how and when to
release it without letting callers keep stale

23
00:00:54.351 --> 00:00:54.751
handles.

24
00:00:54.831 --> 00:00:57.578
Unsafe code should be small, justified,
documented, and tested.

25
00:00:57.658 --> 00:00:59.905
Safety comments should explain ownership
transfer,

26
00:00:59.985 --> 00:01:02.582
allocation and deallocation contracts,
callback lifetime,

27
00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:05.709
pointer validity, synchronization, and
what callers must not violate.

28
00:01:05.789 --> 00:01:08.536
A vague comment that says the block is
safe is not enough evidence.

29
00:01:08.616 --> 00:01:11.612
A safe wrapper should protect ordinary
safe callers from stale access.

30
00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:15.039
It should encode ownership and lifetime
rules in types and runtime checks where

31
00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:15.568
possible.

32
00:01:15.648 --> 00:01:18.245
It should prevent callers from using
handles after shutdown,

33
00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:20.423
passing temporary buffers to retained
pointers,

34
00:01:20.503 --> 00:01:22.900
or depending on external state that may
already be freed.
